("22. Final Acceptance Certificate, Contract Closure and Project Closeout Process",
 "After the DLP is completed and the contractor is released from warranty obligations, the project enters the Final Acceptance Certificate and Contract Closeout Phase. This stage formalizes the complete closure of the EPC contract, releases all financial securities, processes final retention payments, and archives all project records. Complete and orderly project closeout is important for the contractor's financial health, for the DISCOM's asset records, and for the contractor organization's internal learning and performance review processes.",
 [
  ("22.1. Final Inspection – Project Closure Verification Stage",
   "Before the FAC is issued, the DISCOM conducts a final inspection of the distribution infrastructure to confirm its ongoing satisfactory condition at the end of the DLP period. The inspection verifies that the network is in good operational order, no outstanding defects exist, all DLP-period replacements and rectifications are completed, and all documentation submitted during the DLP is complete and accurate."),
  ("22.2. Final Punch Closure – Last Pending Issue Resolution Stage",
   "Any minor residual items identified during the final inspection are documented in a closing punch list. The contractor closes all items within the agreed timeline. The DISCOM confirms closure of each item before proceeding with FAC issuance. This step ensures no deficiencies are inherited by the DISCOM's O&M team without being recorded and resolved."),
  ("22.3. Parallel Closure Activities – Administrative and Financial Closure Workstreams",
   "While the final inspection process is progressing, multiple administrative and financial closure activities begin in parallel. FAC Application is prepared compiling all required documents including the DLP completion record, final defect register, and contractor's declaration of completion. Final Financial Reconciliation verifies all invoice and payment records, confirms advance recovery is complete, calculates the final retention amount due for release, and settles any pending variation order claims. PBG and BG Return Requests are submitted to the DISCOM requesting release of the Performance Bank Guarantee and any outstanding Bank Guarantees. Final Documentation Archive submission provides the DISCOM with any outstanding documentation updates required after the DLP period. All these activities proceed simultaneously to complete the closure efficiently."),
  ("22.4. FAC Issued – Final Acceptance Certificate Approval",
   "After the final inspection is satisfactory, all punch items are closed, and the DISCOM's review of closure documents is complete, the DISCOM issues the Final Acceptance Certificate (FAC). The FAC is the most significant contractual milestone in the distribution project lifecycle — it formally declares that all contractual obligations have been fulfilled, the project work meets the specified quality standards, and the DISCOM unconditionally accepts the completed distribution infrastructure. The FAC triggers the release of the remaining retention amount held since the PAC."),
  ("22.5. Retention Money Release and BG Return – Financial Settlement",
   "Following FAC issuance, the DISCOM processes the release of the balance retention amount held from the contractor's payments since the PAC. The Performance Bank Guarantee and any remaining Bank Guarantees are returned by the DISCOM to the contractor. Final payment is processed for the released retention amount. The contractor reconciles all financial records to confirm the contract is fully settled with no outstanding claims or counterclaims."),
  ("22.6. Internal Project Closeout – Organizational Learning",
   "The project management team prepares an internal Project Closeout Report documenting the complete project execution history. This report covers actual versus planned schedule performance with explanation of key variances, actual versus budgeted cost with analysis of key variances, quality performance including number of defects, rework items, and DLP defects by category, procurement performance including vendor delivery reliability and quality, HSE performance including incident statistics and safety compliance, and key lessons learned with recommendations for future similar projects. Resources are formally demobilized and the project archive is closed."),
  ("22.7. Project Closed – Complete Distribution EPC Lifecycle Closure",
   "After all financial settlements are complete, all documentation is submitted and archived, and the internal closeout report is filed, the project is formally closed. The system status updates to Project Closed, and the distribution EPC lifecycle for this project is complete. The successfully completed project becomes part of the organization's credentials for future bids on similar distribution infrastructure projects."),
 ]),

